The function under test is open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." Reply A is: ```json
{"draft": true} prose
``` (with no text outside the fence). Reply B is: No JSON here. The rule requires A to score the fenced object {"draft": true}, because the fence holds that object even though prose follows it inside the fence. The rule requires B to raise RecordError, because it contains no JSON object. The check, however, gives the same answer to both: it raises RecordError for A as well, because it treats the entire fence content as one candidate, and "{\"draft\": true} prose" is not valid JSON, so the fenced pool yields nothing and there is no top-level object. I would be wrong if the check returned {"draft": true} for A, or if the rule were read to require that a fence "holds one" only when its entire content is exactly one JSON object with no trailing prose.